Out on the high desert highway
Where the terminals hum and the night winds blow
A man came riding from Basecamp country
With a quiet look and a fire below
They said his name was D-H-H
Rails in his blood and a plan in his hand
He didn’t come begging permission
He came to settle the last free land
Oh the Year of Linux kept slipping away
Like smoke on a mesa at break of day
But he packed an opinion, sharp as a knife
And he called that opinion Omarchy for life
The old machines sat fat in their saddles
Mac in the parlor, Windows in town
Folks swore the range would stay fenced forever
And a free man’s desktop would never come down
He didn’t waste words in the saloon light
Just tiled the windows and tuned the keys
Hyprland singing like a taut guitar string
Arch underneath like the bones of the trees
Oh the Year of Linux kept slipping away
Like smoke on a mesa at break of day
But he packed an opinion, sharp as a knife
And he called that opinion Omarchy for life
Twenty good outfits had tried that trail
Twenty good outfits had turned for home
They left their configs scattered like tumbleweed
And the prophecy still rode out alone
Then he dropped an ISO like a challenge in dust
Ten million behind him when the patrons rode in
Dell and the Collisons, Fried and the rest
And the ranger just grinned and tightened the cinch
It ain’t for the timid, it ain’t for the tame
You learn the hotkeys or you learn some shame
But if you love a computer that answers to you
There’s a beautiful system waiting to prove it true
Now the Year of Linux don’t look so far
There’s light on the ridgeline, there’s code in the star
He didn’t wait polite for the world to agree
He built Omarchy and he set it free
So if you see a stranger with a calm, hard smile
And a desktop that moves like a well-trained horse
That’s the ranger from Basecamp, still riding the mile
Making Linux a homeland, not a last resort
